#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161862 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162062 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52596d3a-a7af-55a0-97a8-8a6158b65638" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Variance of a Sum of Scaled Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "1df04c71-caae-5a7e-83ac-4dd578b7f766" :question/math-academy-id "e-9122" :question/problem "For two independent random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\textrm{Var}[X]=10$ and $\\textrm{Var}[Y]=12.$ Find $\\textrm{Var}\\left[X-2Y\\right].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX+bY]=a^2\\textrm{Var}[X]+b^2\\textrm{Var}[Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}\\left[X-2Y\\right]=\\left(1\\right)^2\\cdot 10+\\left(-2\\right)^2\\cdot 12 =58.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161867 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162067 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0df88138-7309-5a09-9a96-5e26029f0186" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Combined Variance Given Some Raw Moments"}] :question/id #uuid "0c3690cd-f90e-5d82-ba7b-09d52de47b80" :question/math-academy-id "e-9143" :question/problem "Consider two independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $\\textrm E[X]=5,$ $\\textrm E[Y]=4$\nand second raw moments $\\textrm E[X^2]=30$ and $\\textrm E[Y^2]=18.$ Find the variance of  $12X-11Y.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX+bY]=a^2\\textrm{Var}[X]+b^2\\textrm{Var}[Y].\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula \n\n$$\n\\textrm {Var}[X]=\\textrm E[X^2]-\\textrm E[X]^2.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $\\textrm{Var}[X]\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =30-{5}^{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =18-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[12X-11Y] & ={12}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+(-11)^{2}\\text{Var}[Y] \\\\  & =144(5)+121(2) \\\\  & =962.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161868 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162068 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e3dad37-612c-56f1-a571-360fafe336b4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Variance of a Sum or Difference of Independent Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "70881840-32f1-5bab-947d-00d77f0ff71e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9181" :question/problem "For two independent random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\textrm{Var}[X]=3$ and $\\textrm{Var}[Y]=7.$ Find $\\textrm{Var}[X-Y].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X\\pm Y]=\\textrm{Var}[X]+\\textrm{Var}[Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X-Y]= 3 + 7 = 10.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162062 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52596d3a-a7af-55a0-97a8-8a6158b65638" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Variance of a Sum of Scaled Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "358167dd-33af-5cd8-a801-68d55f10f6e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427767} {:answer/id #uuid "378c1305-5bb6-5ae2-9992-aaed44785d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427770} {:answer/id #uuid "750f8c08-74db-5e5b-8774-d1122132abe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427786} {:answer/id #uuid "c0542ce2-d653-5ca6-87ce-1e309beec81a" :answer/type {:db/i